## Summary

The `applyOptimisticUpdate` callback in `useOptimisticStatusUpdate.ts` reads the `issues` Map from its closure (lines 142, 159) instead of using the functional updater form of `setIssues`. When two rapid drag-and-drop status changes occur on different issues, the second call sees a stale `issues` Map that doesn't include the first optimistic update. This causes the first update to be silently overwritten. `rollbackUpdate` and `applyServerResponse` already correctly use `setIssues(prev => ...)`.

## Technical Approach

Convert `applyOptimisticUpdate` to use the functional updater form `setIssues(prev => ...)` so each call reads the most recent state. React calls functional updaters synchronously during setState, so we can extract the snapshot via a local variable in the outer scope — the same pattern used in many React codebases.

This also removes `issues` from the `useCallback` dependency array, which improves memoization stability (fewer unnecessary re-creations of `updateIssueStatus`).

## Files to Modify

### `third_party/beads/examples/beads-web-ui/frontend/src/hooks/useOptimisticStatusUpdate.ts`

Replace `applyOptimisticUpdate` (lines 137-166) with a version that uses functional `setIssues`:

**Before (lines 137-166):**
```ts
const applyOptimisticUpdate = useCallback(
  (issueId: string, newStatus: Status, oldStatus: Status): IssueSnapshot | null => {
    let snapshot: IssueSnapshot | null = null;
    const issue = issues.get(issueId);
    if (!issue) return null;
    snapshot = {
      issue: { ...issue },
      oldStatus,
      newStatus,
    };
    const updatedIssue = produce(issue, (draft) => {
      draft.status = newStatus;
      draft.updated_at = new Date().toISOString();
    });
    const newIssues = new Map(issues);
    newIssues.set(issueId, updatedIssue);
    setIssues(newIssues);
    return snapshot;
  },
  [issues, setIssues]
);
```

**After:**
```ts
const applyOptimisticUpdate = useCallback(
  (issueId: string, newStatus: Status, oldStatus: Status): IssueSnapshot | null => {
    let snapshot: IssueSnapshot | null = null;

    setIssues((prev) => {
      const issue = prev.get(issueId);
      if (!issue) return prev;

      snapshot = {
        issue: { ...issue },
        oldStatus,
        newStatus,
      };

      const updatedIssue = produce(issue, (draft) => {
        draft.status = newStatus;
        draft.updated_at = new Date().toISOString();
      });

      const next = new Map(prev);
      next.set(issueId, updatedIssue);
      return next;
    });

    return snapshot;
  },
  [setIssues]
);
```

Key changes:
1. Read `issue` from `prev` (the updater argument) instead of the closure `issues`
2. Build `next` Map from `prev` instead of `issues`
3. Return `prev` unchanged (no-op) if issue not found
4. Remove `issues` from deps array — only `setIssues` is needed

Also update the `updateIssueStatus` useCallback deps (line 296) to remove `applyOptimisticUpdate`'s implicit `issues` dependency — but since `applyOptimisticUpdate` itself is in deps and its identity now only changes when `setIssues` changes, this is already correct.

