Add inline tab renaming to TerminalTabBar: double-click a tab label to enter edit mode with an inline text input. Enter confirms, Escape cancels, blur auto-saves. The rename calls `onTabRename(tabId, newLabel)`, a new callback prop. The parent wires this to `useTerminalMetadata.updateLabel()` (from task .12) which PATCHes the Redis-backed API. Optimistic UI update happens at the hook level. This is a pure UI feature within the TerminalTabBar component — no backend changes needed (task .12 provides the API).

## Technical Approach

**Pattern:** Follow the existing `EditableTitle.tsx` pattern — `isEditing` state triggers a conditional render swapping the label text for an `<input>`. Key differences from EditableTitle: (1) edit mode is triggered by double-click (not single-click), (2) the input is embedded inline within the existing tab element, (3) editing state is local to the component (no `isSaving` prop needed since the parent handles the async call after the callback returns).

**Why double-click instead of single-click:** Single-click already switches the active tab. Double-click is the established OS/IDE convention for renaming (VS Code, Chrome tabs, file explorers). No gesture conflict.

**Why inline input instead of contentEditable:** contentEditable has poor cross-browser behavior for single-line editing, paste handling, and selection. A regular `<input>` is simpler, more predictable, and follows the EditableTitle pattern already in the codebase.

**State management:** `editingTabId: string | null` state inside TerminalTabBar tracks which tab (if any) is in edit mode. `draftLabel: string` holds the input value. Only one tab can be edited at a time.

